module

public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.RelativeProjClosedSubspaceIdeal
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Modules.IdealSubsheafLocal

/-!
# The graded ideal generated in one degree, for a closed subspace of a relative Proj

Let `S` be a scheme, `X` an algebraic space over `S` and `𝒜` a quasi-coherent graded
`𝒪_X`-algebra — the relative Proj situation of Stacks 0849 — and let `π : P = Proj_X(𝒜) ⟶ X` be
the relative Proj.  Stacks 085M asserts, for `X` quasi-compact and quasi-separated, `π`
quasi-compact and `i : Z ⟶ P` a closed subspace of finite presentation, that there are a degree
`d > 0` and a quasi-coherent finite type `𝒪_X`-submodule `ℱ ⊆ 𝒜_d` with
`Z = Proj_X(𝒜/ℱ𝒜)`.

This file writes down the graded ideal `ℱ𝒜` that statement speaks of, degree by degree, and
proves the step of the source's proof which says that `Z` is contained in `Proj_X(𝒜/ℱ𝒜)`:
whenever every local section of `ℱ` is an equation for `Z`, so is every local section of `ℱ𝒜`.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.presheafDegreeProducts`: the objectwise submodule of `𝒜_{d+e}`
  spanned by the products `a · b` of a local section `a` of `ℱ ⊆ 𝒜_d` with a local section `b`
  of `𝒜_e`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.closedSubspaceDegreeProducts`: **the graded ideal `ℱ𝒜` generated by
  `ℱ ⊆ 𝒜_d`**, in degree `d + e`: the smallest submodule of `𝒜_{d+e}` with local membership
  containing those products.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ClosedSubspaceDegreeQuotientPresentation`: the assertion
  `Z = Proj_X(𝒜/ℱ𝒜)` of Stacks 085M, as hypothesis data.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mul_mem_closedSubspaceDegreeProducts` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.closedSubspaceDegreeProducts_le`: `ℱ𝒜` contains every product of a
  section of `ℱ` with a section of `𝒜`, and is the smallest submodule with local membership that
  does, so it is the graded ideal generated by `ℱ` and not some larger submodule.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.closedSubspaceDegreeProducts_mono`: the ideal grows with `ℱ`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.closedSubspaceDegreeProducts_le_ker`: **the containment
  `Z ⊆ Proj_X(𝒜/ℱ𝒜)` of Stacks 085M**, in graded-ideal form.  If `ψ : 𝒜 ⟶ ℬ` is a degreewise
  map of graded algebras which is multiplicative in the bidegree `(d, e)`, and if `ψ` kills every
  local section of `ℱ ⊆ 𝒜_d`, then it kills every local section of `ℱ𝒜` in degree `d + e`.

## Implementation notes

**Why the ideal is indexed by `e` rather than by the total degree.**  The homogeneous product of
the graded algebra has type `𝒜_i ⟶ 𝒜_j ⟶ 𝒜_{i+j}`, so the products of sections of `ℱ ⊆ 𝒜_d`
with sections of `𝒜_e` are sections of `𝒜_{d+e}`.  Indexing the degree-`(d + e)` piece of `ℱ𝒜`
by `e` therefore needs no transport along `d + (n - d) = n`, and nothing is lost: the positive
part of a nonnegatively graded algebra is covered by the degrees `d + e` with `e ≥ 0`, and in
degrees below `d` the ideal generated in degree `d` is zero.

**Why the ideal is a closure and not an objectwise span.**  A section of `𝒜_{d+e}` which is
locally a sum of products `a · b` need not be such a sum globally, so the objectwise span
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.presheafDegreeProducts` is only a submodule of the underlying presheaf
of modules: its membership condition is stable under restriction but not local.  The ideal is its
closure `Stacks.SheafOfModules.sheafifySubmodule`, the smallest submodule of the *sheaf* `𝒜_{d+e}`
containing it, exactly as the product of a sheaf of ideals with a module is built in
`StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Modules.IdealSubsheaf`.  That closure is what makes
`closedSubspaceDegreeProducts_le_ker` a statement with content: the kernel of `ψ` has local
membership, so a section of `ℱ𝒜` that is only locally a sum of products is still killed.

**The multiplicativity hypothesis.**  `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra` carries no notion of
homomorphism in this tree, and the quotient data of Stacks 085L,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ClosedSubspaceQuotientPresentation`, carries only the degreewise maps
`𝒜_n ⟶ ℬ_n` and no compatibility with the products.  So the one bidegree of multiplicativity the
argument uses is a hypothesis of `closedSubspaceDegreeProducts_le_ker` and a field of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ClosedSubspaceDegreeQuotientPresentation`.  It is the defining property
of a map of graded algebras and not an assumption about `Z`.

## What is not proved

**The existence of the degree `d > 0` and of the finite type submodule `ℱ` is not proved, and
nothing here claims it.**  The source gets them by choosing an affine surjective étale chart
`U ⟶ X` (Stacks 03H6, available here as
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.SmallEtale.exists_affineObject_coversTop`), applying the case of
schemes (Stacks 0802,
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjGradedIdeal.exists_finiteType_degreeQuotientPresentation`)
over `U`, and then enlarging the resulting chart-level submodule to a finite type quasi-coherent
submodule of `ℐ_d` on `X` by the colimit clause of Stacks 0829.  That last step is the one this
tree cannot take: the colimit clause,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isColimit_finiteTypeQuasicoherentSubobjectCocone_of_07V9`, still rests
on Stacks 07V9, and the direct alternative fails — the submodule of a module on the small étale
site generated by finitely many sections over an object `U` is stable under restriction along
*every* arrow into `U`, so it is not generated on the slice at `U` by those sections and is not
of finite type for that reason.  `Z = Proj_X(𝒜/ℱ𝒜)` is recorded as hypothesis data because the
statements below predate the construction of `Proj_X(𝒜/ℐ)` on an algebraic space.  That
construction is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjSituation.quotient` with its closed
immersion `…quotientProjHom`, and
`…nonempty_closedSubspaceDegreeQuotientPresentation_of_range_eq`
(`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.RelativeProjClosedSubspaceQuotientPresentation`) builds
the data below from an equality of ranges against it, so an instance now carries the clause
rather than assuming it.

## References

